{"id": 28, "ts": 1779198949.528866, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I continue to favor **Candidate\u202fC** as the provisional best fit for the long\u2011distance pilot role.\n**Key evidence supporting C** \u2013 100\u202f% reliability, ability to make correct decisions quickly, and a talent for creating a positive atmosphere with the crew; also \u201cputs concern for others above everything,\u201d which aids crew cohesion.\n**Evidence against C** \u2013 difficulty communicating ideas, an egocentric style, and a lack of willingness to pursue further education, all of which could hinder coordination on complex, extended flights.\n**Major alternatives** \u2013\n- **Candidate\u202fA** offers excellent situational awareness (anticipates danger, excellent spatial vision) and strong leadership, but is unfriendly, unorganized, resistant to criticism, and has unhealthy habits, which risk flight\u2011planning quality and crew morale.\n- **Candidate\u202fB** is very conscientious, handles stress well, and assesses weather expertly, yet is pretentious, often uses the wrong tone, and can be uncooperative, raising crew\u2011teamwork concerns.\n- **Candidate\u202fD** concentrates well, solves problems, and takes responsibility, but is seen as arrogant, a loner, moody, and has weak leadership, also problematic for crew interaction.\n**Main trade\u2011off** \u2013 C\u2019s reliability and rapid decision\u2011making versus his communication weakness; the other candidates each have stronger situational or technical\u2011type traits but present more serious teamwork or organizational deficits.\n**Remaining uncertainty** \u2013 No candidate has any documented technical piloting competencies (navigation, instrument rating, flight\u2011planning). The real impact of C\u2019s communication difficulty on crew performance is unclear.\n**What would change my vote** \u2013 Concrete evidence that another candidate possesses strong, documented t